  Of course Digicash won't replace government money overnight, however,
over time transactions will gradually become electronic. An increasing
amount of business in conducted in the US electronically. Credit cards,
wired money, the world currency exchange market, etc.

  Let me see if I can explain Digicash to you. (BTW, Digicash, Inc is a company
which is already attempting to set up a cryptographic electronic money
system).

  The scheme is based on Public-key encryption technology. Public Key
encryption allows one to publish a key so other people can send secret
data to you. The most important thing about this system is that only
YOU can decrypt the information with your secret key. Unlike other
cryptosystems where knowing the encryption key ('password') allows you
to decrypt the ciphertext, public-key encryption allows the entire
world to send you secret data and only you are able to decrypt it.
A side benefit of public-key technology is that one can digitally "sign"
some data and others can verify that signature. Signatures work by
instead publishing the decryption key, and keeping the encryption key
secret. I could encrypt a messsage saying "I am Ray" and others could
check that I was the author of that message by attempting to decrypt it
with the decryption key associated with me. Since only *I* have the
encryption key, only I could have written a message which could be decrypted
by my publically available decryption key.

  So how is Digicash secure and untraceable?

  I walk into my favorite cryptobank and hand the teller $100 cash to be
deposited into an account (they don't have to know my name or anything).
I then request $20 in digicash notes. The bank teller asks me to make
up a random number for the serial # on my $20 bank note. The teller covers
her eyes while I type my secret random number into her computer and press
enter. The computer encrypts my secret random number with the bank's 
encryption key and deposits it onto my cash-card. (the act of the
bank encrypting my cash's serial number is done in such a way that the
bank cannot discover the number by decrypting it. This is all accomplished
mathematically and is provably secure [the NSA can't even get around it])
Finally, the bank subtracts $20 from my accont.

  Later on, after having dinner in a restaurant, a cashier asks me to pay
my bill. Their sign says "we accept Mastercard, Visa, American Express, or
Digicash". I hand him my digicash card and he attempts to decrypt a $20
encrypted bank note with the cryptobank's public decryption key. It succeeds
and his computer calls up the bank's computer and gives it my digicash
note. The bank decrypts the note (verifying to the bank that it issued
the note and isn't counterfeit.) and extracts the serial number on the
note. The bank then tells the restaurant's computer "ok" and credits it $20.
The bank marks the serial number as "spent". (the purpose of this is to
prevent me from spending the same banknote twice)

  I walk out of restaurant satisfied and happy that the bank doesn't
know where I spent my $20.

  Q: Doesn't the bank know that you spent your money at the restaurant?
  A: No. A huge bank would be issue millions of 20 dollar electronic bills.
     Since the bank doesn't see my secret random serial number when they
     encrypt it, they don't know which account the serial number is associated
     with. When the restaurant sends the banknote to the bank, the only
     information the bank has is the serial number and the restaurant.
     They know that one of X million people spent $20, but which one.

  Q: How does the bank encrypt the serial number you generate without
     seeing it?
  A: The serial number is first multiplied by another random number called
     the "blinding factor". After the bank encrypts it, you "divide" it
     out of the bank note. The bank only gets to see the product, not the
     factors.

  Q: How will bank notes be distributed?
  A: Most likely on some kind of anonymous cash smartcard. That is, a card with
     no particular owner, but the ability to store and retrieve binary data.
     The notes will probably be spent over a computer network, much like
     modern ZOM machines verify credit cards at stores.

  Q: What about collaboration between the store owner and bank.
  A: Unless the store asks for identification, they don't know anything.


  Additional benefits:

 Digicash provides a perfectly secure wallet. By encrypting the bank notes
with your own p-key, no thief on earth (even the government!) could use 
them. Stealing cash would become an obsolete crime. Thieves wouldn't even
know which bank contains your real checking account and neither would
the bank who issued the note itself!
    
 So if the DEA breaks into your house and steals your cash card, who cares.
They can't spend the money, don't know where it's really located at, and
the card is just a conveinent notepad. You could broadcast your banknotes
(encrypted) to the entire internet for safekeeping and no one could spend
them but you (provided you can remember your secret key).

  In summary, not only is digital cash NOT tracable, it provides benefits
that far outweigh other types of money.

  That's all for now. I'm starting to get sleepy. Your best best would be
to purchase Extropy #10 and read Hal Finney's excellent article on
digicash.




    






